# CONTRACTS — the map, never the copy

A **contract** is an iron rule that must never be violated except by an explicit human decision.
Contracts are scattered *by necessity* — each is enforced where it lives (a DB constraint, a code
module, a `contract-*` doc) — so this register is the **single map**: it **links** to each
contract's source and **never copies its text**. A contract with no row here is invisible; a row
that restates its source is a copy that will drift.

Curated by the steward; agents propose rows. Establishing a contract = **enforce it at its source**
*and* add one row. Changing a contract = change its **source** — and **a `contract-*` document is
changed only by the user**: the class is in the name, agents propose (the five system contracts in
`.critos/system/` are the same class one tier up — installed, changed only by a release).

**What earns a row — the placement test** (`contract-shared § 3` holds the full form): only a
contract **mandatory at project level or binding more than one agent across a boundary**. Split a
compound statement into atoms first, then per atom: already covered by a law above (check its
**scope**, not its subject) → cite the law, no row · the user's **choice** → `settings.md` keeps it ·
an enforced rule → the row maps the **enforcement source** (never a definition, never the owner's
own memory), and a single-domain rule stays in its owner's docs. Both will *and* enforced → the
source holds the operative text, this register maps it, settings keeps the choice.

**Sections are named for the DOMAIN, never for an actor** — who owns a domain is the roster's
business. **A row that leaves, leaves a TOMBSTONE** in its own section naming where the rule now
lives — the rule still binds; only its tier moved. Tombstones get cited: a wrong departure reason
teaches wrong law forever.

## Legend

**Level** — where the rule bites, strongest first: `DB` (the schema refuses) · `build` (a build
step refuses to ship) · `code` (a module of record refuses or encodes it) · `run` (the pipeline
stamps or flags at run time) · `doc` (a named `contract-*` doc or convention — **tier 3**:
enforced by being read; the linked source is the home).

**Override** — `No` (structural) · `User` (only the user, usually a migration) · `Yes` (a
documented opt-out exists).

**Blocks / flags** (guards only) — a guard's **teeth**, never implied by its Level: `BLOCKS` (the
violation cannot proceed) · `FLAGS` (it proceeds and announces itself). A banner followed by exit
`0` is `FLAGS`.

